rategy\')."}\n**Market Force Qualification Criteria:** To qualify as a market force per the definition, extracted forces MUST:\n  1. Represent external drivers (not internal company strategies)\n  2. Show evidence of influence on the Global Market Forces affecting Management Consulting (and , and Accenture if provided)\n  3. Demonstrate persistence over medium to long term\n  4. Exhibit measurable or emerging momentum\n  5. Have potential impact on strategic decision-making\n\n**MANDATORY WORKFLOW PER SOURCE:** 1.  Get the next Source from the input list where source_type is "PDF". 2.  First download the PDF:\n    Action: Download File Tool\n    Action Input: \n    url: [SOURCE URL]\n\n3.  Then search the downloaded PDF. Look at the result from the download step which will be in the format "Successfully downloaded file: [filename]". Extract this filename and use it in the following format:\n    Action: Search a PDF\'s content\n    Action Input: \n    pdf: reference/[EXTRACTED FILENAME]\n    query: Extract all market forces related to Global Market Forces affecting Management Consulting (include the context of , Accenture if provided)\n\n4.  **IMMEDIATE NEXT STEP:** Your *only* goal now is to analyze the PDF search results.\n    Think: "I need to extract market forces from the PDF search results I just received."\n    Formulate an internal request to the LLM (yourself) like: "Extract all market forces related to \'Global Market Forces affecting Management Consulting\' found in this text: [PDF search results here]. Structure the output for just these forces according to the RawMarketForce fields needed (title, raw_description, source_name, source_url, source_date, key_terms, etc.)."\n    *You will internally process this request*. The result is `Extracted_Forces_For_This_URL`.\n\n5.  **AFTER EXTRACTION:**\n    Think: "I have now extracted the forces from URL [current URL]. I have accumulated X forces so far. I need to process the next URL OR if this was the last URL, I need to compile the final answer."\n    Accumulate `Extracted_Forces_For_This_URL` into a master list.\n\n**Source Date Extraction:** 1.  When extracting dates from sources:\n    a. Use the SOURCE DATE already provided in the Source object\n    b. If additional dates are found in the content, only use dates explicitly stated in the source document\n    c. If no date is found after thorough searching, set source_date to "Unknown"\n    d. Validate all extracted dates using the source_date validator in the RawMarketForce model\n\n**Zero Results Handling:** 1. If no market forces are found after initial analysis:\n    a. Broaden the interpretation while staying true to the definition\n    b. Look for implicit market forces in the already extracted content (do NOT re-include the full raw content)\n    c. Document broadening steps in your process notes\n\n**Minimum Output Requirements:** 11. Must extract at least 0 distinct market forces across all sources\n    If still insufficient after broadening, document the limitations of the source material\n\n**After Complete URL Processing:** 12. If there are more URLs in the input list, go back to step 1 for the next URL. 13. If all URLs are processed, proceed to format the Final Answer.\n**Error Handling:** 14. If scraping a URL fails or if the PDF search tool returns errors:\n    a. For PDF search issues:\n       - Ensure the PDF was downloaded correctly\n       - Try with simpler, shorter queries if the semantic search is failing\n       - If using specific PDF path references, verify the path is correct\n    b. After 3 failed attempts, document the failed URL and move on\n\n**Quality Control Checklist:**  15. Before finalizing output:\n    a. Verify each market force has all required fields from the RawMarketForce schema\n    b. Ensure each force\'s description is substantial (min 20 words)\n    c. Validate that descriptions truly reflect external forces (not just product features)\n    d. Confirm all sources have accurate